{The parent starts with member 8, prioritizes dispersion-triggered selections of members 6 and 5, and otherwise switches from member 8 to member 9 when candidate availability is low or operation-count progress is high. The main modification replaces only that abrupt 8-to-9 transition with a continuous two-member mixture, retaining the first-step choice and dispersion overrides: member 8 has a cubic processing-efficiency penalty, whereas member 9 uses a squared penalty and a different waiting scale. The mixture gradually shifts toward member 9 as either original switching boundary approaches; this is an unevaluated design hypothesis, not a claim of improved fitness.}
